em this tool  so once they trained a new model they're  gonna see this they're gonna see if  they're missing something if they're not  behaving somehow and then we also gave  him another tool because we felt like  that wasn't enough our tool the first  tool you saw was for each model on its  own but this tool allows them to choose  a model according to their own decision  so what you see on the y-axis is the  false amiss and emission rate disparity  given two different groups you divide  the false emission rate between the two  of them you want the value to be as  close to one as possible and then you  look at the evaluation metric in our  case it's precision so in general you  want to be on the y axis as close to one  as possible and on the x axis as much to  the right as possible again close to one  so here in this image you'll want the  orange ones that are on the top right  these three both have good false  emission rate disparities  they don't really differentiate between  female and male but also they have quite  good precision rates so this allows that  our clients to actually take a look and  understand for themselves what is their  decision and make a more informed  decision at that so they see this each  dot is a model they see it for males and  females but then they also see that  according to race and as you can see  according to race is a bit noisier and  now it's actually the same two oranges  and one orange but you can see that the  two oranges actually have a much better  false disparity at false emission rate  disparity than the one that was earlier  on top and then you'd probably want to  use those too so this gives them a  better understanding of which model they  should actually use so in summary what  we did is we converted a reactive system  of mental health outreach to a proactive  one we use machine learning for that  obviously we gave a club a closed system  for non CS clients and trust me this was  a hard task because explaining to them  wh